    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A Southern Pacific Railroad depot opened in 1873, sparking El Monte’s growth in fruit orchards and walnut fields.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/El-Monte-California-United-States"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1849 a stage station appeared, and by 1852 adobe homes and a schoolhouse stood on the site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/El-Monte-California-United-States"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Long before the Spanish, Gabrielino natives discovered abundant water and wood at the El Monte site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.elmontehistory.org/el-monte-history"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1849‑50 Rush brought settlers to El Monte, turning it into an oasis.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.elmontehistory.org/el-monte-history"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "By the 1900s El Monte blossomed into a hub of fruit orchards, walnut groves, truck farms, hay fields, and dairies.",
